    {
      "slug": "cc-him-on-everything",
      "script": "CC him on everything? My new regional manager announced that every email, no matter how small, needed him CC'd—no exceptions, so he could stay in the loop. I complied. I CC'd him on lunch orders. I CC'd him when the printer jammed. I even CC'd him on a reply-all thanking someone for coffee. Within four days, his inbox had over eleven hundred unread messages, and he missed the actual urgent email about a shipment delay, buried under three hundred about bagels. I hope he liked bagels. He called an emergency meeting to demand better judgment about what counted as necessary. I quoted his no-exceptions policy in front of everyone. He changed the policy within the hour. Funny how fast judgment returns when the inbox revolt reaches its author. He wanted every email—still?",
      "editor_note": "Rewrote to 134 words, preserved all eight shot phrases and protected facts, added a dry bagel aside, and turned the policy reversal into a specific question; repaired sentence starts for the named-entity guard."
    },
    {
      "slug": "he-faked-our-own-flyer",
      "script": "Forty dollars off—again? At the hardware store, a regular tried to price-match a power drill using a photo of a competitor's flyer. He insisted their price was forty dollars less. I matched it without arguing—store policy. A week later, he returned with the same flyer for another tool, then one with the same font and same crooked blue sale banner for a third item. I zoomed in on the competitor's logo. It was ours, mirrored and recolored in red. He agreed. He'd been editing our weekly ads and claiming they came from across town. I quietly printed every transaction from the past two months, checked our sale calendar, and gave the folder to my manager. He got banned from price-matching for a year. He still shops, paying full price loudly. Was the fake red logo worth a year?",
      "editor_note": "Rewrote to 140 words, preserved all eight shot phrases and protected facts, sharpened the forged-logo reveal, and closed on the one-year ban; repaired the sentence start for the named-entity guard."
    }
  ],
